// Practice quiz: Role-protected endpoints
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/api-security/role-protected-endpoints

::o1 matching::How does authorizeHttpRequests choose a rule for a request? {
~The most specific rule automatically#Not quite. Rules are not sorted for you.
~The last matching rule#Not quite. The first match wins.
=The first matching rule, in order#Correct. So specific rules come first.
~A random rule#Not quite. It is deterministic.
}

::o1 default::Why end with anyRequest().authenticated()? {
~It disables CSRF#Not quite. It is unrelated.
=Any new endpoint is protected by default#Correct. Forgetting a rule does not leave it open.
~It speeds up requests#Not quite. It is a rule, not an optimization.
~It allows anonymous calls#Not quite. It requires authentication.
}

::o2 401::A request has no credentials for a protected endpoint. What status results? {
~403#Not quite. That is for a known caller without permission.
~200#Not quite. It is protected.
=401#Correct. The caller is not authenticated.
~404#Not quite. The path exists.
}

::o2 403::A valid user lacks the required role. What status results? {
~401#Not quite. The caller is authenticated.
~200#Not quite. The role is required.
~500#Not quite. That is a server failure.
=403#Correct. Authenticated, but not allowed.
}

::o3 testing::Which cases should a protected endpoint test cover? {
=Anonymous, wrong role and right role#Correct. Each proves a different rule behavior.
~Only the admin case#Not quite. That misses accidental openings.
~Only the anonymous case#Not quite. That misses role checks.
~None if the code compiles#Not quite. Compiling proves nothing here.
}

